in professional workflows.

1000
00:47:27,120 --> 00:47:29,580
We have covered some of
the agentic workflows

1001
00:47:29,580 --> 00:47:31,323
in early parts of the lecture.

1002
00:47:31,323 --> 00:47:32,740
And it's definitely
cost effective

1003
00:47:32,740 --> 00:47:34,407
when you couple it
with human oversight,

1004
00:47:34,407 --> 00:47:36,900
like if the humans
are architecting what

1005
00:47:36,900 --> 00:47:39,820
the models need to go work on,
the AI assistance is definitely

1006
00:47:39,820 --> 00:47:41,220
helping.

1007
00:47:41,220 --> 00:47:46,808
In certain occupations, this
is more impactful than not.

1008
00:47:46,808 --> 00:47:48,600
But you do have to
choose different models,

1009
00:47:48,600 --> 00:47:51,860
and different models are
stronger at different task types

1010
00:47:51,860 --> 00:47:53,620
as well.

1011
00:47:53,620 --> 00:47:58,200
OK, questions or comments.

1012
00:48:02,020 --> 00:48:04,020
Yes.

1013
00:48:04,020 --> 00:48:11,220
How much [INAUDIBLE] on the
actual dollar value [INAUDIBLE].

1014
00:48:11,220 --> 00:48:12,260
So the tasks?

1015
00:48:12,260 --> 00:48:15,632
Yeah.

1016
00:48:15,632 --> 00:48:18,380
Why do you ask that question?

1017
00:48:18,380 --> 00:48:25,860
Well, because it seems like it's
a relevant way of quantifying

1018
00:48:25,860 --> 00:48:27,160
actual economic impact.

1019
00:48:27,160 --> 00:48:32,880
You can say, oh, AI can now do
$100,000 of work or something

1020
00:48:32,880 --> 00:48:34,420
that.

1021
00:48:34,420 --> 00:48:38,140
But if these numbers
are collected

1022
00:48:38,140 --> 00:48:46,860
in a way that seems unreliable
or misleading, then [INAUDIBLE].

1023
00:48:46,860 --> 00:48:49,540
I think to me, it's not
so much about the dollar.

1024
00:48:49,540 --> 00:48:53,380
I mean, they did go after
the top 5% of the GDP tasks.

1025
00:48:53,380 --> 00:48:57,908
For the past several
decades, the knowledge work

1026
00:48:57,908 --> 00:48:59,700
has been one of the
key areas that has been

1027
00:48:59,700 --> 00:49:00,975
the highest paid profession.

1028
00:49:00,975 --> 00:49:03,100
And in knowledge work,
there are certain categories

1029
00:49:03,100 --> 00:49:06,340
of tasks that the models
will be very good at.

1030
00:49:06,340 --> 00:49:09,740
Deep research being one of
them, perhaps, another one

1031
00:49:09,740 --> 00:49:11,160
being like code generation.

1032
00:49:11,160 --> 00:49:13,820
So in each-- or
understanding a diverse

1033
00:49:13,820 --> 00:49:15,480
set of information sources.

1034
00:49:15,480 --> 00:49:18,002
So the models will generally
be good at those set of tasks.

1035
00:49:18,002 --> 00:49:19,460
So I think the
question I would ask

1036
00:49:19,460 --> 00:49:23,585
is, what is the category of
tasks that the models are

1037
00:49:23,585 --> 00:49:24,460
likely to be good at?

1038
00:49:24,460 --> 00:49:26,428
Just because at any
given point in time,

1039
00:49:26,428 --> 00:49:28,220
humans are strong at
certain set of things.

1040
00:49:28,220 --> 00:49:30,380
And machines, just
by way of being

1041
00:49:30,380 --> 00:49:34,718
able to crunch the
electricity, are just better

1042
00:49:34,718 --> 00:49:35,760
at certain set of things.

1043
00:49:35,760 --> 00:49:38,120
So this is more
along those lines.

1044
00:49:38,120 --> 00:49:43,260
So there's a certain category
of tasks that will definitely

1045
00:49:43,260 --> 00:49:44,460
be valuable.

1046
00:49:44,460 --> 00:49:46,920
Delegating to the model
is the way to look at it.

1047
00:49:46,920 --> 00:49:49,640
And which are the professions
in which those category of tasks

1048
00:49:49,640 --> 00:49:50,140
fall?

1049
00:49:50,140 --> 00:49:54,660
Is what this benchmark
is showcasing.

1050
00:49:54,660 --> 00:49:57,060
Before this benchmark
came out, people

1051
00:49:57,060 --> 00:49:59,533
would talk about this
area of like OK, this

1052
00:49:59,533 --> 00:50:01,200
is going to impact a
lot of professions,

1053
00:50:01,200 --> 00:50:02,580
but there was no
quantitative way

1054
00:50:02,580 --> 00:50:05,400
to measure what set of tasks
in each of these professions.

1055
00:50:05,400 --> 00:50:07,920
And it seemed like a
humongous task to go do that.

1056
00:50:07,920 --> 00:50:10,860
So this is a very
verifiable, quantifiable way

1057
00:50:10,860 --> 00:50:14,995
to even have a taxonomy
to list the set of tasks

1058
00:50:14,995 --> 00:50:16,120
in each of the professions.

1059
00:50:16,120 --> 00:50:19,420
And what does it mean for
the model to go do them?

1060
00:50:19,420 --> 00:50:22,260
Earlier, when we
were doing the class,

1061
00:50:22,260 --> 00:50:24,020
in the previous
iteration, we were only

1062
00:50:24,020 --> 00:50:26,980
talking about ML-Bench and
RE- Bench and given that.

1063
00:50:26,980 --> 00:50:29,980
Our backgrounds are, say, more
ML or more research or more

1064
00:50:29,980 --> 00:50:30,940
software.

1065
00:50:30,940 --> 00:50:35,140
It's easier for us to understand
the progress of these models

1066
00:50:35,140 --> 00:50:36,520
in those domains.

1067
00:50:41,140 --> 00:50:44,540
I'm just curious, how is
[INAUDIBLE] when the model will

1068
00:50:44,540 --> 00:50:49,340
outperform the
human, it just means

1069
00:50:49,340 --> 00:50:54,980
like it's human, not
necessary [INAUDIBLE] anymore.

1070
00:50:54,980 --> 00:50:57,400
I mean, this is where I
was adding these caveats.

1071
00:50:57,400 --> 00:50:59,880
So when a model is
outperforming the human,

1072
00:50:59,880 --> 00:51:02,000
you should go look
at the prompt.

1073
00:51:02,000 --> 00:51:04,580
And what that's saying
is that for a human who

1074
00:51:04,580 --> 00:51:07,300
has a lot of
context, if they were

1075
00:51:07,300 --> 00:51:09,020
to specify all the
context in their head

1076
00:51:09,020 --> 00:51:11,940
to specify it in a
prompt, then the models

1077
00:51:11,940 --> 00:51:15,300
can also do those tasks.

1078
00:51:15,300 --> 00:51:20,020
While a lot of actual work
experience or a lot of workplace

1079
00:51:20,020 --> 00:51:23,320
challenges often are about
figuring out what to prioritize,

1080
00:51:23,320 --> 00:51:25,460
what are the set of
problems to solve,

1081
00:51:25,460 --> 00:51:27,520
where to put our resources.

1082
00:51:34,180 --> 00:51:37,900
We will move to the last
benchmark, which will be

1083
00:51:37,900 --> 00:51:40,340
focused on DeepScholar-Bench.

1084
00:51:40,340 --> 00:51:42,660
This was a task that
one of our classmates

1085
00:51:42,660 --> 00:51:46,300
requested early on, where they
want AI to write the literature

1086
00:51:46,300 --> 00:51:48,220
review section of their paper.

1087
00:51:48,220 --> 00:51:49,560
So here we are.

1088
00:51:49,560 --> 00:51:52,620
Let's benchmark that task.

1089
00:51:52,620 --> 00:51:54,120
This was written at Stanford.

1090
00:51:54,120 --> 00:51:58,020
And this is basically
measuring the question of,

1091
00:51:58,020 --> 00:52:03,700
instead of asking how long
will the task take to solve,

1092
00:52:03,700 --> 00:52:05,120
is the model output good enough?

1093
00:52:05,120 --> 00:52:08,680
They're actually asking, can AI
perform deep research synthesis?

1094
00:52:08,680 --> 00:52:10,900
Can I go study a
bunch of references

1095
00:52:10,900 --> 00:52:13,160
and retrieve the
relevant information

1096
00:52:13,160 --> 00:52:17,020
and then synthesize the findings
into a coherent set of reports

1097
00:52:17,020 --> 00:52:19,080
and have verifiable citations?

1098
00:52:19,080 --> 00:52:23,060
And there have been
several industry prototypes

1099
00:52:23,060 --> 00:52:23,880
that have come out.

1100
00:52:23,880 --> 00:52:26,240
So there's OpenAI deep
research, Gemini deep research.

1101
00:52:26,240 --> 00:52:28,680
Perplexity has a
deep research thing.

1102
00:52:28,680 --> 00:52:30,320
Storm is from Stanford.

1103
00:52:30,320 --> 00:52:32,580
There is OpenScholar.

1104
00:52:32,580 --> 00:52:35,420
You folks built a deep
research agent in homework 3

1105
00:52:35,420 --> 00:52:38,500
and maybe you got a flavor
of what that feels like.

1106
00:52:38,500 --> 00:52:40,900
So in general, there has been
a lot of research activity

1107
00:52:40,900 --> 00:52:43,460
in this area, and it still
continues to be a challenging

1108
00:52:43,460 --> 00:52:45,120
area, as what this paper shows.

1109
00:52:49,140 --> 00:52:51,940
So the task that
this paper addresses

1110
00:52:51,940 --> 00:52:55,600
is to generate the related work
section for academic papers.

1111
00:52:55,600 --> 00:52:57,100
The reason they
care about this task

1112
00:52:57,100 --> 00:53:00,180
is because this is a
task that most of us

1113
00:53:00,180 --> 00:53:02,420
require when we
are writing papers.

1114
00:53:02,420 --> 00:53:04,960
And it requires continuous
updates with fresh queries.

1115
00:53:04,960 --> 00:53:07,060
So there is not no staleness.

1116
00:53:07,060 --> 00:53:09,780
The way they went about
constructing this dataset

1117
00:53:09,780 --> 00:53:13,900
is they used recent arXiv
papers with PhD level difficulty

1118
00:53:13,900 --> 00:53:15,660
across 22 domains.

1119
00:53:15,660 --> 00:53:20,760
And they rerun it monthly with
new papers, so it stays live.

1120
00:53:20,760 --> 00:53:23,760
So it's very nice in
that respect as well.

1121
00:53:23,760 --> 00:53:26,120
And it avoids data
contamination.

1122
00:53:26,120 --> 00:53:27,940
They only look at
papers that are

1123
00:53:27,940 --> 00:53:32,040
post-training of a major model.

1124
00:53:32,040 --> 00:53:35,220
So that's very nice.

1125
00:53:35,220 --> 00:53:37,260
What they're evaluating
the models on

1126
00:53:37,260 --> 00:53:38,857
is three particular axes.

1127
00:53:38,857 --> 00:53:40,940
So the first thing they're
evaluating the model on

1128
00:53:40,940 --> 00:53:41,997
is knowledge synthesis.

1129
00:53:41,997 --> 00:53:43,580
So what they're
basically saying that,

1130
00:53:43,580 --> 00:53:46,820
is the model output
organizing and is

1131
00:53:46,820 --> 00:53:49,520
coherent in organizing the
knowledge that is presenting?

1132
00:53:49,520 --> 00:53:51,400
And is it capturing
all the key facts?

1133
00:53:51,400 --> 00:53:54,500
So is it capturing
the key nuggets?

1134
00:53:54,500 --> 00:53:58,180
This second axes, it's
evaluating the model

1135
00:53:58,180 --> 00:53:59,900
on its retrieval quality.

1136
00:53:59,900 --> 00:54:03,020
So if it's looking at a
particular set of references,

1137
00:54:03,020 --> 00:54:07,160
how relevant they are to what
you wanted to do research on?

1138
00:54:07,160 --> 00:54:09,100
For example, if you're
writing a related work

1139
00:54:09,100 --> 00:54:15,200
section for your final
project, and you have say,

1140
00:54:15,200 --> 00:54:20,480
10 references that this model
or agent went and fetched,

1141
00:54:20,480 --> 00:54:22,580
are they relevant
to what you actually

1142
00:54:22,580 --> 00:54:25,980
want to present in your
final report or not?

1143
00:54:25,980 --> 00:54:27,620
Citation counts.

1144
00:54:27,620 --> 00:54:30,480
It's kind of well known that you
want highly authentic sources.

1145
00:54:30,480 --> 00:54:33,180
So whether the papers
are important in terms

1146
00:54:33,180 --> 00:54:35,780
of covering those
in the retrieval

1147
00:54:35,780 --> 00:54:37,620
and then reference coverage.

1148
00:54:37,620 --> 00:54:39,220
So this is similar to--

1149
00:54:39,220 --> 00:54:42,420
is it finding important
papers or not?

1150
00:54:42,420 --> 00:54:46,650
And then the third axis it's
measuring is verifiability.

1151
00:54:46,650 --> 00:54:49,490
So, do the citations
support the claims?

1152
00:54:49,490 --> 00:54:52,790
So even if it finds
good papers and it's

1153
00:54:52,790 --> 00:54:55,070
citing some aspect
of that paper,

1154
00:54:55,070 --> 00:54:58,270
is the claim supported
by that citation?

1155
00:54:58,270 --> 00:55:00,880
And then, are claims
backed by the citation?

1156
00:55:00,880 --> 00:55:02,130
So these two are very similar.

1157
00:55:02,130 --> 00:55:06,310
But one is measuring precision
and one is measuring coverage.

1158
00:55:06,310 --> 00:55:10,890
And each of these metrics are
actually validated by humans.

1159
00:55:10,890 --> 00:55:14,230
So they got about 70% to
80% of human agreement

1160
00:55:14,230 --> 00:55:18,790
when they got humans
to evaluate this.

1161
00:55:18,790 --> 00:55:21,182
Now the fun thing is that
most benchmarks get saturated.

1162
00:55:21,182 --> 00:55:23,390
So when we look at benchmarks,
we are like, OK, well,

1163
00:55:23,390 --> 00:55:25,410
it's already at 70% or 80%.

1164
00:55:25,410 --> 00:55:26,450
So there is no headroom.

1165
00:55:26,450 --> 00:55:28,325
The fun thing about this
particular benchmark

1166
00:55:28,325 --> 00:55:32,290
is that none of the existing
systems actually exceeds 19%.

1167
00:55:32,290 --> 00:55:34,350
So if this were to show
up in your homework

1168
00:55:34,350 --> 00:55:36,050
for the next iteration
of the class,

1169
00:55:36,050 --> 00:55:37,675
there's a lot of
headroom for you folks

1170
00:55:37,675 --> 00:55:40,590
to actually make
it a final project.

1171
00:55:40,590 --> 00:55:44,750
In terms of, if you look at
each of these axes on which they

1172
00:55:44,750 --> 00:55:46,930
are breaking down the
model performance,

1173
00:55:46,930 --> 00:55:48,630
in terms of knowledge
synthesis, they

1174
00:55:48,630 --> 00:55:51,390
found that OpenAI deep research
does a pretty good job,

1175
00:55:51,390 --> 00:55:55,368
but almost all of the models
consistently miss key facts.

1176
00:55:55,368 --> 00:55:56,910
So even though they
write coherently.

1177
00:55:56,910 --> 00:55:59,590
So it's great English,
but not necessarily

1178
00:55:59,590 --> 00:56:01,870
covering all the key facts.

1179
00:56:01,870 --> 00:56:07,070
The retrieval quality
tends to be overall OK.

1180
00:56:07,070 --> 00:56:09,750
So all systems
currently struggle

1181
00:56:09,750 --> 00:56:12,870
to find comprehensive and
important set of sources.

1182
00:56:12,870 --> 00:56:17,670
The document importance in all
of them is less than 12.5%.

1183
00:56:17,670 --> 00:56:20,970
And then finally, if you
look at verifiability,

1184
00:56:20,970 --> 00:56:27,250
they found that say DeepScholar
base got up to 90% precision.

1185
00:56:27,250 --> 00:56:29,850
But OpenAI deep research
has lower verifiability,

1186
00:56:29,850 --> 00:56:31,830
despite the fact that it
does a really good job

1187
00:56:31,830 --> 00:56:34,490
at writing really nice English.

1188
00:56:34,490 --> 00:56:40,830
So the synthesis is
extremely coherent.

1189
00:56:40,830 --> 00:56:42,490
Now, if you look at
the failure modes,

1190
00:56:42,490 --> 00:56:46,230
and that's where a lot
of the learnings lie.

1191
00:56:46,230 --> 00:56:48,710
Typically, the failure
modes come from not

1192
00:56:48,710 --> 00:56:50,450
finding comprehensive sources.

1193
00:56:50,450 --> 00:56:52,890
So even when they do
find relevant documents,

1194
00:56:52,890 --> 00:56:55,050
they're not always finding
the foundational papers.

1195
00:56:55,050 --> 00:56:57,370
So this is where if you
are an expert in the area,

1196
00:56:57,370 --> 00:56:59,750
you kind of know from
experience that there

1197
00:56:59,750 --> 00:57:02,190
are all these foundational
papers because you

1198
00:57:02,190 --> 00:57:07,110
build that context and bookmark
all the important papers.

1199
00:57:07,110 --> 00:57:09,930
The deep research agents
do a reasonably strong job,

1200
00:57:09,930 --> 00:57:12,830
but they struggle to assess
the document importance

1201
00:57:12,830 --> 00:57:16,550
beyond whether it's relevant
to the context or not.

1202
00:57:16,550 --> 00:57:17,815
The surface essential facts.

1203
00:57:17,815 --> 00:57:19,190
So even with good
retrieval, they

1204
00:57:19,190 --> 00:57:22,930
don't always extract the
key information efficiently.

1205
00:57:22,930 --> 00:57:25,950
If you do give the
perfect sources,

1206
00:57:25,950 --> 00:57:28,190
so even if you say that
OK, here are all the papers

1207
00:57:28,190 --> 00:57:29,730
that should have been included.

1208
00:57:29,730 --> 00:57:33,070
Even then, if you try to get
them to extract the key facts,

1209
00:57:33,070 --> 00:57:34,950
even then they get
about 50% coverage.

1210
00:57:34,950 --> 00:57:39,230
And without knowing the exact
papers, they get lower coverage.

1211
00:57:39,230 --> 00:57:41,670
And then finally, if you're
trying to balance out

1212
00:57:41,670 --> 00:57:44,990
the synthesis quality
versus verifiability,

1213
00:57:44,990 --> 00:57:47,230
none of the systems
actually excels

1214
00:57:47,230 --> 00:57:50,270
at both the quality of
synthesis and the verifiability

1215
00:57:50,270 --> 00:57:51,350
in certain ways.

1216
00:57:51,350 --> 00:57:56,590
So you can see that being able
to refer to reference data

1217
00:57:56,590 --> 00:58:00,250
in the agentic evaluation
tasks is a non-trivial problem.

1218
00:58:00,250 --> 00:58:02,230
So it's not just about
how long the task is

1219
00:58:02,230 --> 00:58:04,770
or whether it's real world
economic value or not.

1220
00:58:04,770 --> 00:58:07,830
If you have to reference some
reference data to go look at,

1221
00:58:07,830 --> 00:58:11,790
it's non-trivial because you
might not get the context

1222
00:58:11,790 --> 00:58:12,730
that an expert has.

1223
00:58:12,730 --> 00:58:14,990
So you might not have that
much foundational knowledge

1224
00:58:14,990 --> 00:58:15,790
available.

1225
00:58:15,790 --> 00:58:18,190
It might not extract the
key information well enough,

1226
00:58:18,190 --> 00:58:19,990
so it does something.

1227
00:58:19,990 --> 00:58:22,750
So it's good with
human AI assistance.

1228
00:58:22,750 --> 00:58:25,630
And the verifiability
with respect to citations

1229
00:58:25,630 --> 00:58:28,190
may not have the right precision
recall trade-off that's

1230
00:58:28,190 --> 00:58:30,250
acceptable for that occupation.

1231
00:58:33,590 --> 00:58:37,310
So that roughly means-- so this
is transitioning to summarize

1232
00:58:37,310 --> 00:58:38,650
across all the papers.

1233
00:58:38,650 --> 00:58:41,910
But before I go there, let
me open up for questions just

1234
00:58:41,910 --> 00:58:46,910
on the DeepScholar-Bench
style of tasks.

1235
00:58:46,910 --> 00:58:48,450
Any questions or comments?

1236
00:58:53,390 --> 00:58:55,150
Yeah.

1237
00:58:55,150 --> 00:58:58,750
[INAUDIBLE] seems like a much
more specific narrow thing

1238
00:58:58,750 --> 00:59:01,490
than the other two.

1239
00:59:01,490 --> 00:59:04,950
Why talk about this one
in particular as opposed

1240
00:59:04,950 --> 00:59:08,430
to any other [INAUDIBLE]?

1241
00:59:08,430 --> 00:59:10,750
So I think the broad
theme of the lecture

1242
00:59:10,750 --> 00:59:15,310
